course: dictionary
lesson: 10067
title: Texture: how things feel
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Texture has more than one meaning, and the clues in the sentence tell you which one. «Every kitchen is a chemistry lab: heat, acid and water change food into new substances with new tastes, smells and textures.» Here the textures are of food, and food is tasted and eaten, so texture means how food feels in your mouth.
explain: «The ridges help you grip and help you feel textures, because stroking a surface makes tiny vibrations that nerves pick up.» The word feel is the clue. Texture is how a surface feels when you touch it, such as rough, smooth, bumpy or soft.
explain: Ask yourself: is someone touching it, tasting it, or looking at how its parts are arranged? A good check is to swap in the word feel or surface and see if the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Texture is how something feels when you touch it, such as rough, smooth, bumpy or soft.
fact: Texture is also how food feels in your mouth, like crunchy, chewy or creamy.
fact: To texture something means to give it a rough or patterned surface.
fact: «Soil texture depends on how much sand, silt and clay it has»; loam, a balanced mix, is the best for farming.
fact: «Octopus skin» can raise bumps to copy the texture of rock.
exercise: «Every kitchen is a chemistry lab: heat, acid and water change food into new substances with new tastes, smells and textures.» In this sentence, what are textures? | The names of the cooks | The loud noise of the cooker | The shapes of the kitchen tiles | *How food feels in your mouth
exercise: Which of these is a texture you can feel by touch? | *Bumpy | Sour | Tuneful | Bright
exercise: To texture something means to | Paint it one single colour | Melt it into a liquid | *Give it a rough or patterned surface | Measure how heavy it is
test: «Soil texture depends on how much sand, silt and clay it has.» What does soil texture depend on? | The colour of the sky | How many worms live in it | *How much sand, silt and clay it has | The weather on the day
test: «Octopus skin can raise bumps to copy the texture of rock.» Why does an octopus raise bumps? | To swim faster | *To copy the texture of rock | To hear better | To make its skin look blue
exam: Ella says texture means colour. Why is that wrong? | Texture means a loud noise | Texture means the size of something | Texture means colour, so she is right | *Texture is how something feels, not what colour it is
